# Copy to .env and fill in. This is THE answer file: the app's configuration
# AND the single-node deployment's (deploy/single/, the section at the bottom)
# — one file, because the operator's stated failure mode was drift between two
# files holding the same fact (design record
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-23-airgap-check-configure-setup-design.md, D1).
# Almost every var is prefixed CC_; the handful that are not are named for the
# container that reads them and say so.
#
# The deploy scripts SOURCE this file (`set -a; . .env`), so QUOTE any value
# containing a space or a shell metacharacter — see CC_FEED_QUERY below for the
# one shipped example. python-dotenv/pydantic-settings strip the quotes on read.
#
# 127.0.0.1, never localhost: Windows resolves localhost to ::1 first and the
# podman machine publishes IPv4-only — measured 2s/connection of pure loss
# (2026-08-21 Windows validation, W1).

# --- Database (the framework spine) ---
# Port 5442 is Central Command's own Postgres (docker-compose.yml). Do NOT point this
# at 5432 — that is n8n's `nat-postgres`.
CC_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://central_command:central_command@127.0.0.1:5442/central_command

# --- THE LLM provider agents talk to (live mode) ---
# Presence is enablement: no on/off flag, no fallback chain. Set these two and
# that is where every live agent call goes; base_url is always pinned, so an
# ambient ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL can never redirect it. Missing either one raises in
# live mode rather than half-configuring a client — point it at something that
# is down and runs fail loudly. This picks ONE destination; retry/fallback
# across models is LiteLLM's own config, not Central Command's.
# Default = the self-hosted LiteLLM proxy's Anthropic-native passthrough
# (/v1/messages), which preserves the tool-call shapes the proposal spine parses.
# To bypass LiteLLM, set CC_LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com and put a
# real Anthropic key in CC_LLM_API_KEY.
CC_LLM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4000
# A LiteLLM VIRTUAL key scoped to Central Command (never the master key) — or an
# Anthropic key if the base URL above is Anthropic.
CC_LLM_API_KEY=
# Provider:model string for Pydantic AI. The model name is sent as-is, so it
# doubles as the LiteLLM alias. Default to `cc-default`: Central Command names an
# intent and LiteLLM decides what it resolves to, so the model behind it can be
# changed in the proxy with no edit here and no restart.
CC_DEFAULT_MODEL=openai:cc-default
# OPTIONAL per-agent virtual keys (Phase 5 attribution): name them
# CC_LLM_PROXY_KEY_<AGENT_ID> (agent_id upper-cased, '-'→'_'). When set, that
# agent authenticates against the SAME base URL with its own LiteLLM key, so
# spend attributes per agent; unset agents use CC_LLM_API_KEY. Provision by
# creating the key (via the manager or UI), capturing its plaintext once, and
# pasting here.
# CC_LLM_PROXY_KEY_INBOX_TRIAGE=
# CC_LLM_PROXY_KEY_JIRA_EXPERT=
# CC_LLM_PROXY_KEY_AUDITOR=
# CC_LLM_PROXY_KEY_LITELLM_MANAGER=
# OPTIONAL per-agent MODEL overrides: CC_MODEL_<AGENT_ID>, same provider:model
# shape as CC_DEFAULT_MODEL. Lets one agent run on a different LiteLLM alias or
# model while the rest stay on the default.
# CC_MODEL_INBOX_TRIAGE=openai:cc-default

# --- The LiteLLM deployment we ADMINISTER (litellm-manager, D23) ---
# A different fact from CC_LLM_BASE_URL above: the manager agent needs a LiteLLM
# address + master key to read models/spend and to perform approved model
# changes, whether or not agent traffic routes through that same proxy.
CC_LLM_PROXY_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4000
# LiteLLM MASTER key — the admin credential the Executor holds for the
# LiteLLM-manager agent's approved model changes (add/delete) and its reads.
# Powerful (proxy-wide); unset = the manager's reads/writes fail loudly. On the
# single-node profile this IS the proxy's LITELLM_MASTER_KEY (compose passes it
# under that name) and the LiteLLM admin UI's password; generated by
# deploy/single/make-secrets.sh when blank.
CC_LLM_PROXY_ADMIN_KEY=
# Browser-reachable LiteLLM UI URL (tailnet), optional — cockpit shows a "View in LiteLLM" link when set.
CC_LLM_PROXY_UI_URL=
# Anthropic key the EXECUTOR injects into approved `anthropic/...` model
# registrations in LiteLLM, so a re-registered model still authenticates.
# NOT a routing credential — agent traffic uses CC_LLM_API_KEY above.
CC_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Autodiscovery reads credentials straight out of LiteLLM's OWN Postgres — the
# operator's only enrollment step is adding a credential in the LiteLLM UI.
# 5443 = the litellm-db loopback hostPort (deploy/k3s/30-litellm.yaml,
# CC_LITELLM_DB_PORT in the deployment section). On the single-node profile
# the `app` phase DERIVES this line from LITELLM_POSTGRES_PASSWORD and
# CC_LITELLM_DB_PORT — leave the CHANGEME marker and it will be filled in.
CC_LITELLM_DB_URL=postgresql://llmproxy:CHANGEME@127.0.0.1:5443/litellm
# LiteLLM's LITELLM_SALT_KEY — decrypts credential_values read from the above,
# and on the single-node profile it IS the value the proxy container is given.
# NEVER change it after first run: it encrypts every stored virtual key.
# Generated by deploy/single/make-secrets.sh when blank.
CC_LITELLM_SALT_KEY=

# The embedder the curation writer (integrations/neo4j_writer) uses: the
# LiteLLM ALIAS and the vector dimension the Neo4j indexes were created with.
# Must equal the graphiti config's embedder block — a mismatched dimension is
# a corrupt index, not an error, and it is effectively permanent (changing
# the embedder = drop the index + re-embed the graph). Defaults below are the
# homelab instance; the single-node profile uses cc-embedding.
#
# CC_EMBED_DIM IS MEASURED, NEVER DECLARED. `./deploy/single/setup.sh llm`
# embeds one string through the cc-embedding alias, counts the vector, and
# writes the number here; it REFUSES to overwrite a different value that is
# already set, because the number is written into the Neo4j vector index and
# changing it means dropping the index and re-embedding the whole graph.
# deploy/single/compose.yaml reads it for the graphiti container, so the
# `stack` phase refuses to create the graph until it has been measured.
# Leave both commented unless you know the answer.
# CC_EMBED_ALIAS=qwen3-embedding-local
# CC_EMBED_DIM=1024

# How charters address the human team lead — filled into <<operator_name>>
# when a charter template renders at hire. Set by the setup interview; the
# default keeps pre-onboarding hires reading "the operator".
# CC_OPERATOR_NAME=the operator

# --- MCP endpoints (existing homelab services) ---
CC_GRAPHITI_MCP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000/mcp

# --- Knowledge graph tenanting ---
# Reads span the preserved homelab graph ("main") + Central Command's own group;
# approved episodes are written ONLY to Central Command's group.
CC_GRAPH_READ_GROUPS=main,central_command
CC_GRAPH_WRITE_GROUP=central_command

# --- API ---
CC_API_HOST=0.0.0.0
CC_API_PORT=8080

# --- Calendar façade (EA) ---
# cc-calendar-facade wraps the operator's Google Calendar; the OAuth credential
# stays in n8n. That credential is full-scope — read-only is enforced by the
# façade workflow's mode gate, not by the credential. Empty token = the EA's
# brief simply carries no calendar.
CC_CALENDAR_FACADE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:5678/webhook/cc-calendar-facade
CC_CALENDAR_FACADE_TOKEN=
# The operator's timezone: the EA's calendar day is a LOCAL day, not a UTC one.
CC_EA_TIMEZONE=America/Denver

# --- Web fetch (D-web-read) ---
# Ungated read, granted only via the `web-read` pack. Client-PKI (mTLS) is ONE
# outbound identity for every fetch this process makes, never a per-agent
# argument. PEM paths only — a corporate PKCS#12 bundle needs converting first:
#   openssl pkcs12 -in id.p12 -out id.pem -nodes
# Cert/key paths must be readable by the user running cc-uvicorn.
CC_FETCH_CLIENT_CERT=
CC_FETCH_CLIENT_KEY=
CC_FETCH_CA_BUNDLE=
# Scope the cert: comma-separated host suffixes it may be presented to
# (e.g. "corp.example.com,tools.internal"). Empty = every host — fine on the
# homelab; SET THIS when the cert is a corporate identity.
CC_FETCH_CERT_HOSTS=
CC_FETCH_TIMEOUT=30.0
CC_FETCH_MAX_BYTES=2000000
# Brave Search key for the `web-search` pack. Empty = search is unavailable and
# `search_web` says so; agents fall back to operator-supplied URLs + fetch_url.
#CC_BRAVE_API_KEY=

# --- Email façade + live feed (M12) ---
# cc-email-facade wraps lib-email-provider in n8n; Gmail creds stay in n8n.
CC_EMAIL_FACADE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:5678/webhook/cc-email-facade
CC_EMAIL_FACADE_TOKEN=
# The feed poller is opt-in and the QUERY is the volume knob — keep it narrow.
CC_FEED_ENABLED=false
CC_FEED_POLL_SECONDS=60
# QUOTED on purpose: the value contains a space, and `set -a; . .env` (what
# every deploy script does) would otherwise try to run `newer_than:1d` as a
# command. python-dotenv/pydantic-settings strip the quotes on read.
CC_FEED_QUERY="in:inbox newer_than:1d"
# Backlog sweep cutoff: a fixed DATE (per deployment), never a duration.
# Mail older than this is never processed. Empty = the sweep refuses to start.
CC_BACKLOG_CUTOFF_DATE=

# --- Ingestion dispatcher (Phase 2) — the backpressure valves ---
# Off by default: the drain loop should never autostart and quietly burn tokens.
CC_DISPATCH_ENABLED=false
CC_DISPATCH_CONCURRENCY=1
# Pause dispatch while this many proposals await review — don't outrun the human.
CC_DISPATCH_APPROVAL_LIMIT=3
CC_DISPATCH_TOKEN_BUDGET=0
CC_DISPATCH_POLL_SECONDS=2.0
CC_DISPATCH_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
CC_DISPATCH_STALE_CLAIM_SECONDS=900

# Auditor (Phase 4, D8/D12) — independent re-verification of no-action claims.
# shadow = record verdicts beside each dismissal, every gate unchanged;
# active  = auto-confirm CONCURRED dismissals (never operator-reopened items).
CC_AUDITOR_ENABLED=false
CC_AUDITOR_MODE=shadow
# CC_AUDITOR_MODE governs the EMAIL class only; document dismissals graduate on
# their own evidence, under their own class and this knob (same two values).
CC_AUDITOR_DOCUMENT_MODE=shadow

# Stale-page annotation (sources-catalog slice 7, Decision 9) — its own action
# class, its own rung of the same ladder.
# off  = staleness reaches Confluence only through gated repair proposals;
# auto = the freshness sweep writes the bounded stale panel itself, from claim
#        rows, with no model in the loop (actor system:wiki-freshness).
CC_WIKI_ANNOTATION_MODE=off

# The ATTENTION BUDGET (teaming doctrine, Decision 4): how many times a day the
# executive assistant may OPEN A CONVERSATION with you unprompted. Enforced
# mechanically at the lane-opening seam, not asked for in a charter. Over
# budget the ask degrades to a queued question you can still answer, and the
# next digest's window covers the gap. 0 = unlimited; every other agent is
# already unlimited, because their asks are your own work coming back to you.
# The default errs LOW on purpose — turn it up, not down.
CC_EA_CONTACT_BUDGET_PER_DAY=3

# OUTAGE EQUIVALENCE (2026-07-31): a decision resume that fails because the
# provider was DOWN parks the session AWAITING_RESUME instead of closing it,
# and the resume sweep drives it once the dependency is healthy. This is how
# many times one parked session may be re-driven before the system admits the
# outage outlived its patience: exhaustion promotes to today's terminal
# behaviour PLUS an operator item, so nothing retries forever silently.
CC_RESUME_RETRY_LIMIT=5

# LOOP BREAKER, NOT A BUDGET. Model requests one run may make before it is cut
# off. Tokens are free here (local models) and nothing in this system caps
# spend — what this catches is a run ping-ponging the same tool call forever
# while its session sits RUNNING. Applied at the single run seam, so every path
# (task, ingest, coach, converse, orchestrator, resume) inherits it. Exhaustion
# is SEMANTIC: the session fails loudly with its partial transcript and goes to
# you, because retrying a loop just loops. Raise it freely if real work is
# hitting it.
CC_RUN_REQUEST_LIMIT=50

# ADMISSION CONTROL for model turns, PER BACKEND: alias groups that share one
# backend and how many requests that backend takes at once. `+` joins aliases
# onto one pool, `;` separates pools, `*` is the pool for any alias not named
# (unnamed aliases are unlimited without it). A local llama-server with
# `--parallel 1` queues FIFO, so more than one in-flight turn just waits, times
# out at the proxy, and leaves the GPU grinding the abandoned request — name
# every alias that lands on it and give the group 1. Hosted APIs need nothing.
# Example: CC_MODEL_CONCURRENCY=cc-default+gpt-4.1-nano=1
CC_MODEL_CONCURRENCY=

# Native Jira client (D23) — set both to cut over from the n8n façade;
# unset to fall back. Token: id.atlassian.com → Security → API tokens.
CC_JIRA_EMAIL=
CC_JIRA_API_TOKEN=
# API flavor: "cloud" (default — REST API v3, ADF rich text) or "server"
# (Data Center/Server — REST API v2 only, wiki-markup rich text, offset
# search, and no filter-search/dashboard/gadget endpoints; the packs withhold
# those tools). Coded to Atlassian's published Data Center REST reference —
# verify it against your instance with `python scripts/atlassian_probe.py`.
#CC_JIRA_API_FLAVOR=cloud
# Auth mode: empty (default) FOLLOWS THE FLAVOR — "basic" (Cloud: email + API
# token above) under cloud, "bearer" (a PAT in CC_JIRA_API_TOKEN,
# CC_JIRA_EMAIL ignored) under server. Set it explicitly to override; Basic
# works on both products. Same rule for CC_CONFLUENCE_AUTH_MODE below.
#CC_JIRA_AUTH_MODE=

# Git-hosting read integration (`forge-read` pack) — Central Command is a CLIENT
# of GitHub / self-hosted GitLab, never a host. Empty = unconfigured; the
# tools answer honestly rather than erroring. One instance:
#CC_FORGE_INSTANCES=github=github:https://api.github.com
# Several named instances (the work shape — multiple GitLabs + a GitHub mirror):
#CC_FORGE_INSTANCES=github=github:https://api.github.com,work-gl=gitlab:https://gitlab.example.com,mirror-gl=gitlab:https://gitlab-mirror.example.com
# Per-instance PAT, name upper-cased with '-'→'_'. Missing = unauthenticated
# requests (fine for public GitHub reads, subject to rate limits).
#CC_FORGE_TOKEN_GITHUB=
#CC_FORGE_TOKEN_WORK_GL=

# Native Confluence client, Phase 1 read-only (`confluence-read` pack). Same
# giftable convention as CC_JIRA_*: empty by default so a gift install never
# points at the operator's Confluence. Token: id.atlassian.com -> Security -> API
# tokens (Cloud) shares the same token as Jira if it's the same site.
CC_CONFLUENCE_BASE_URL=
CC_CONFLUENCE_EMAIL=
CC_CONFLUENCE_API_TOKEN=
# Auth mode: empty (default) follows CC_CONFLUENCE_API_FLAVOR — "basic"
# (Cloud: email + API token above) under cloud, "bearer" (a PAT in
# CC_CONFLUENCE_API_TOKEN, CC_CONFLUENCE_EMAIL ignored) under server. Set it
# explicitly to override.
#CC_CONFLUENCE_AUTH_MODE=
# API flavor: "cloud" (default) or "server" (Data Center 9.x — REST API v1
# throughout; coded to Atlassian's docs — verify on-site with
# `python scripts/atlassian_probe.py` before trusting it).
#CC_CONFLUENCE_API_FLAVOR=cloud
# Informational only — names the active macro/profile (e.g. a documented space
# layout) so agents know which convention applies. Nothing parses this.
#CC_CONFLUENCE_PROFILE=

# Sandbox slice 1 (D-sandbox): the sandbox-runner is a separate, credential-
# free service (central_command/sandbox/runner.py, its own uvicorn on :8090) that
# drives ephemeral gVisor Jobs on the chromebox by shelling out to kubectl.
# Localhost-only, same trust as CC_API_PORT today.
CC_SANDBOX_RUNNER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8090
# Shared bearer token between the app and the runner. When set, the runner
# 401s any request without it (localhost is a shared trust domain). Generate:
#   python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
CC_SANDBOX_RUNNER_TOKEN=
CC_SANDBOX_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=3600
CC_SANDBOX_EXEC_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=120

# Sandbox slice 2: mcp.sync_source, the one gated exit into servers/<id>/.
# CC_MCP_SERVERS_ROOT empty = "<repo root>/servers".
CC_MCP_SYNC_MAX_BYTES=524288
CC_MCP_SERVERS_ROOT=

# Sandbox slice 3: mcp.build_image (ssh build host, single-arch per §9.3) and
# mcp.server_deploy (scoped kubeconfig from make-mcp-kubeconfig.sh). Both
# refuse loudly, never silently, when left empty.
CC_MCP_BUILD_HOST=chromebox
CC_MCP_DEPLOY_KUBECONFIG=

# litellm.apply_config_change: the scoped kubeconfig from
# deploy/k3s/make-litellm-kubeconfig.sh (SA cc-litellm-operator — one
# ConfigMap, one Deployment, no secrets). Empty or missing = the capability
# refuses BEFORE writing anything.
CC_LITELLM_KUBECONFIG=
# Read-only kubeconfig for litellm_read_logs (runtime tool) — SA
# cc-litellm-logreader — pods/pods-log get+list only. Separate from
# CC_LITELLM_KUBECONFIG above on purpose: the runtime holds READ creds only.
CC_LITELLM_LOG_KUBECONFIG=

# cc-crawler (deploy/k3s/70-crawler.yaml): browser-rendering crawl service for
# JS-rendered docs sites — rung 2 when a plain fetch reads an empty shell. It
# runs on the CHROMEBOX (Chromium is the memory hog the placement rule keeps
# off the Pi) and is reachable here on loopback via ServiceLB. Leave the
# default; not reachable degrades honestly rather than failing the caller.
#CC_CRAWLER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8091

# Cockpit Graph panel (deploy/k3s/cc-graph-bolt.service, 2026-08-09): a
# loopback-ONLY socat relay of svc/neo4j 7687 onto 127.0.0.1, read-only bolt
# reads for central_command/integrations/neo4j_reader.py. Password matches
# CC_NEO4J_PASSWORD, which the neo4j container also reads (as NEO4J_AUTH) on
# the single-node profile — never commit the real value. Generated by
# deploy/single/make-secrets.sh when blank.
#CC_NEO4J_URL=bolt://127.0.0.1:7687
CC_NEO4J_PASSWORD=
# Browser-reachable Neo4j Browser UI URL (tailnet), optional — the Systems
# view shows an "Open →" link when set. Same pattern as CC_LLM_PROXY_UI_URL.
CC_NEO4J_BROWSER_URL=

# Browser-reachable n8n UI URL (tailnet), optional — the Systems view shows
# an "Open →" link when set. Same pattern as CC_LLM_PROXY_UI_URL.
CC_N8N_UI_URL=

# Browser-reachable VictoriaLogs UI URL (tailnet), optional — the Systems
# view shows an "Open →" link when set. Same pattern as CC_LLM_PROXY_UI_URL.
CC_VLOGS_UI_URL=

# Browser-reachable llama-swap Web UI (tailnet), optional — the local-model
# slot LiteLLM routes to; its bundled UI shows loaded models, swap state and
# per-request token counts. The Systems view shows an "Open →" link when set
# and probes the same origin for up/down. Same pattern as CC_LLM_PROXY_UI_URL,
# e.g. http://<compute-host-tailnet-ip>:8081/ui
CC_LLAMA_SWAP_UI_URL=
# Systems-page Swagger links (sandbox runner + crawler /docs) and the database
# UI (Adminer) — display-only, same "unset = no link" rule. Values are browser
# URLs, typically tailscale-serve entries in front of the loopback ports.
CC_SANDBOX_DOCS_URL=
CC_CRAWLER_DOCS_URL=
CC_DB_UI_URL=

# --- Enterprise network: pluggable trust for FIXED integration endpoints -----
# (work-transition compatibility design, decision 3; distinct from the
# CC_FETCH_* identity above, which scopes an agent-named URL's client by
# host.) Applies to Jira, Graphiti's MCP endpoint, and the LiteLLM
# admin/embed/OCR calls — one outbound identity, because each hits one fixed,
# operator-configured endpoint. Empty (default) = unchanged homelab behavior:
# public CA verification, no client cert. PEM paths only (see the pkcs12
# conversion note above); must be readable by the user running cc-uvicorn.
#
# CC_CA_BUNDLE is ONE fact with several consumers: the integration clients
# here, every host-side ACQUISITION the single-node install does (curl, uv/pip,
# npm, node, git — fanned out by deploy/env-lib.sh's cc_export_tls_env, called
# by every command in the profile), the three image BUILDS (as a `--secret`, so
# it never enters a build context or `podman history`), the podman MACHINE's
# trust store (written by `./setup.sh machine`), and the two containers that
# dial outward (LiteLLM, the speech engine — mounted read-only at
# /etc/cc/ca.pem). Set it for a TLS-intercepting proxy or a self-signed mirror.
# The full fan-out table is beside CC_TLS_INSECURE in the deployment section;
# the egress proxy that usually comes with it is CC_PROXY, there too.
#CC_CA_BUNDLE=
#CC_CLIENT_CERT=
#CC_CLIENT_KEY=

# --- Run modes (CLAUDE.md "Run it") -------------------------------------------
# demo_mode: deterministic FunctionModel instead of real Claude.
# executor_mode: dry_run logs writes; live performs them after approval.
CC_DEMO_MODE=false
CC_EXECUTOR_MODE=live

# --- Tunables the code defaults; uncomment to override ------------------------
# Every value below matches central_command/config.py's default — the file is the
# catalogue, config.py is the truth. See each field's docstring for the WHY.
#CC_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=262144
# The voice cc-tts speaks in when the cockpit names none — REQUIRED on the
# wire (LiteLLM's Router.aspeech() and the speaches engine both reject a
# request without one). Default = the bundled Kokoro's first voice.
#CC_TTS_VOICE=af_heart
#CC_DEFAULT_MODEL is above; jira: native client (D23) + n8n facade token
#CC_JIRA_BASE_URL=https://your-org.atlassian.net
#CC_JIRA_FACADE_TOKEN=
# n8n Jira façade webhook (M3) — the fallback path `_or_facade` routes to
# until CC_JIRA_EMAIL/CC_JIRA_API_TOKEN are set; not vestigial, still live.
#CC_N8N_JIRA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:5678/webhook/cc-jira-facade
#CC_BACKLOG_QUERY=in:inbox
#CC_BACKLOG_WINDOW_DAYS=30
#CC_REFLECTION_ENABLED=true
#CC_REFLECTION_MAX_EPISODES=3
#CC_REFLECTION_MIN_TRANSCRIPT_CHARS=2000
#CC_SEMANTIC_FREEZE_ENABLED=true
#CC_SEMANTIC_FREEZE_THRESHOLD=0.70
#CC_HEARTBEAT_ENABLED=false
#CC_HEARTBEAT_TICK_SECONDS=15.0
#CC_ORCH_STALL_LIMIT=2
#CC_ORCH_MAX_ROUNDS=50
#CC_ORCH_FANOUT_LIMIT=25
#CC_GRAPH_AUDITOR_ENABLED=false
#CC_GRAPH_AUDITOR_MODE=shadow
#CC_EXECUTION_RETRY_LIMIT=5
#CC_TASK_RETRY_LIMIT=5
#CC_CONTEXT_WINDOW=200000
#CC_CONTEXT_PRESSURE_THRESHOLD=0.6
#CC_DISCUSSION_STALL_HOURS=24
#CC_CRAWLER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8091

# ===== Deployment — single-node profile (deploy/single/) =====================
#
#   Everything below is read by deploy/single/*.sh and deploy/single/compose.yaml
#   (invoked as `compose --env-file <repo>/.env`), and by deploy/discover.sh.
#   It was its own file, deploy/single/env.example, until v2.42.0; one answer
#   file is the whole point — an existing install's deploy/single/.env is
#   merged in here automatically on the next setup/update run and moved aside.
#   The k3s profile is NOT covered by this section and keeps deploy/pi/.env.
#
#   YOU DO NOT HAVE TO FILL THIS IN BY HAND (v2.45.0). `./setup.sh configure`
#   asks for every answer this file does not carry yet — it is also the one
#   command that CREATES this file from the template — and `./setup.sh check`
#   validates the same list. Both read one schema, deploy/single/questions.tsv,
#   so a key with a row there has a prompt, a default and a validator. Editing
#   this file directly stays fully supported: that is what makes it a preseed
#   file you can fill in on a connected machine and carry across.
#
#   make-secrets.sh GENERATES the credentials left blank (the LiteLLM
#   master/salt keys, the Postgres and Neo4j passwords, the n8n encryption key)
#   and writes them BACK into this file. Two of them must then never change:
#     CC_LITELLM_SALT_KEY  encrypts the stored LiteLLM virtual keys
#     N8N_ENCRYPTION_KEY   decrypts the stored Gmail OAuth credential
#   A restore under a different value leaves the rows present but
#   undecryptable. Back this file up somewhere OUTSIDE the install tree.
#
#   Not repeated here, because they are ONE fact each and already above:
#     CC_LLM_PROXY_ADMIN_KEY  the proxy's master key (container: LITELLM_MASTER_KEY)
#     CC_LITELLM_SALT_KEY     (container: LITELLM_SALT_KEY)
#     CC_NEO4J_PASSWORD       (container: NEO4J_AUTH / NEO4J_PASSWORD)
#     CC_LITELLM_DB_URL       derived by the `app` phase
#     CC_EMBED_DIM            MEASURED, never declared — by `./setup.sh check`
#                             from the upstream embedder when it can reach one,
#                             else by the `llm` phase through the proxy alias.
#                             Written only when unset; a later mismatch FAILS
#     CC_CA_BUNDLE            the private CA, for integrations AND acquisition

# ── Where generated files live (D7) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Logs, diagnostics, installed.manifest, pid files, the updater's working dir
# and discovery's evidence — ALL of it, outside the checkout, so `git status`
# is clean after every command. Blank = the default,
# ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/central-command/<repo-dir>-<hash>,
# which the first run RESOLVES and writes back here (the one .env write a
# read-only command makes, so bash and Python never disagree about the
# spelling of a Windows path). `./setup.sh diagnose` prints it first and
# `./setup.sh status` shows it. The APP reads this key too — the cockpit's
# in-product updater keeps its staged zip and status record there.
CC_STATE_DIR=

# ── The upstream LLM: OPTIONAL here; the LiteLLM UI is the primary way ──────
# Central Command's agents address ONE alias on the local LiteLLM proxy, and the
# proxy's catalog lives in ITS OWN DATABASE — not in this file.
#
# THE PRIMARY METHOD IS THE UI, and it is the same one the k3s profile uses: the
# `llm` phase creates the alias skeletons and PAUSES (exit 3) so you enter
# provider, model id and key at http://127.0.0.1:4000/ui, then you re-run. That
# pause is a DELIBERATE exception to "a full run does not stop" (operator's
# decision), because LiteLLM expresses provider nuance — credentials,
# per-provider parameters, routing, fallbacks — that a flat answer file cannot,
# and one method across both profiles beats two that drift.
#
# EVERYTHING BELOW IS AN OPTIONAL SHORTCUT past that pause (v2.44.0, 2026-09-23
# design record D3; made optional in v2.45.1). It fits one case: a single
# OpenAI-compatible endpoint serving every model, with one base URL, one key and
# one model id per alias. Set them and setup registers real rows itself — and,
# more to the point, `./setup.sh check` proves the endpoint FROM THIS HOST before
# a single container exists, which on an air-gapped install is much earlier than
# the UI pause. Leave them blank (the normal case) and nothing is lost but that
# early proof; `check` says so in a PASS line rather than asking you for them.
#
#   CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_BASE_URL   the /v1 base THIS HOST can reach. A 127.0.0.1
#                              value is rewritten to host.containers.internal
#                              for the LiteLLM row (the row is dialled by a
#                              CONTAINER), with a WARN saying so.
#   CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_API_KEY    its key. Never printed, never logged. A key the
#                              server ignores can be `none`, but it must be
#                              non-empty — LiteLLM needs something to send.
#   CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_MODEL_<A>  the UPSTREAM model id for alias <A> (the name the
#                              server gives the model, not the alias). The key
#                              name is the alias upper-cased with every
#                              non-alphanumeric turned into `_`.
#
# WHICH ALIASES THE DECLARATION MUST COVER **IF YOU USE IT** — one table, and
# `cc_required_aliases` in deploy/env-lib.sh is the code that reads it. (A
# PARTIAL declaration is fine: an alias whose key is blank simply keeps its
# skeleton and is filled in the UI, so the pause returns for that one alias.)
#
#   alias          key                                   covered when
#   cc-default     CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_MODEL_CC_DEFAULT      always  (the spine's
#                                                          one alias: chat)
#   graphiti-llm   CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_MODEL_GRAPHITI_LLM    always  (graph
#                                                          extraction; needs
#                                                          json_schema output)
#   cc-embedding   CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_MODEL_CC_EMBEDDING    always  (its DIMENSION
#                                                          is measured, never
#                                                          declared — see
#                                                          CC_EMBED_DIM)
#   gpt-4.1-nano   CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_MODEL_GPT_4_1_NANO    always  (graphiti_core's
#                                                          reranker addresses
#                                                          that literal name;
#                                                          any chat model does)
#   cc-tts         CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_MODEL_CC_TTS          CC_ENABLE_SPEECH=1
#   cc-stt         CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_MODEL_CC_STT          CC_ENABLE_SPEECH=1
#
# With the BUNDLED speech engine the two speech values are the HF model ids
# below (CC_SPEECH_TTS_MODEL / CC_SPEECH_STT_MODEL) — the engine answers on the
# compose network, so those two rows keep their own api_base; setup prints the
# exact values at the pause. With CC_ENABLE_SPEECH=0 you point both aliases at
# engines of your own in the UI, which is why they are not required here.
#
# List what a server names its models with:
#   CC_LLM_BASE_URL=<url>/v1 CC_LLM_API_KEY=<key> ./deploy/single/discover-llm.sh models
# The curation writer embeds through a LiteLLM ALIAS, so set CC_EMBED_ALIAS=
# cc-embedding above on this profile (the commented default is the homelab's).
#CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_BASE_URL=https://llm.corp.example/v1
#CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_API_KEY=
#CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_MODEL_CC_DEFAULT=
#CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_MODEL_GRAPHITI_LLM=
#CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_MODEL_CC_EMBEDDING=
#CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_MODEL_GPT_4_1_NANO=
#CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_MODEL_CC_TTS=
#CC_LLM_UPSTREAM_MODEL_CC_STT=

# ── Names and ports ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The CONTAINER-name prefix (compose.yaml's container_name). It is no longer a
# DNS name: services find each other by compose SERVICE name — `neo4j`,
# `litellm`, `speech` — and the network is the compose project's own. The
# prefix is what verify.sh, `./setup.sh diagnose` and ./update.sh use to
# address containers, so changing it means changing nothing else, but it must
# not collide with another stack's containers on the same machine.
CC_POD_PREFIX=cc-

# Loopback host ports. The defaults match what the app section above assumes.
CC_PG_PORT=5442
CC_LITELLM_PORT=4000
CC_LITELLM_DB_PORT=5443
CC_GRAPHITI_PORT=8000
CC_NEO4J_BOLT_PORT=7687
CC_NEO4J_HTTP_PORT=7474
CC_N8N_PORT=5678
CC_CRAWLER_PORT=8091
CC_SPEECH_PORT=8093
# The cockpit server (web/server-dist, the k3s cc-nerve unit's job) — boot
# starts it here and points you at it; the API's own port stays CC_API_PORT.
# The launcher EXPORTS this as PORT into the node process (with GATEWAY_URL and
# CC_UPDATE_BACKEND); web/.env is retired on this profile.
CC_COCKPIT_PORT=3080

# ── What to install (setup.sh reads these; 1 = yes, 0 = no) ─────────────────
# n8n is only needed by an n8n-backed integration (today: Gmail). Off by
# default — skip it and the install is two containers lighter.
CC_ENABLE_N8N=0
# The crawl service (Chromium, rung 2 of docs ingestion). Its image is built
# locally and is large; rung 1 (plain HTTP fetch) still works without it.
CC_ENABLE_CRAWLER=1
# The agent sandbox. On this profile its backend is ROOTLESS PODMAN, not the
# k3s profile's gVisor — weaker isolation, stated plainly. The runner itself
# is a host process you start alongside the API (see deploy/single/README.md).
CC_ENABLE_SANDBOX=1
# The speech engine (Speaches: faster-whisper STT + Kokoro TTS) behind the
# cc-tts / cc-stt aliases the cockpit's voice input and read-aloud use. 0 means
# you point BOTH aliases at engines of your own in the LiteLLM UI (e.g. hosted
# Whisper-convention models) — the aliases are required either way.
CC_ENABLE_SPEECH=1
# The two HF-hub model ids the llm phase installs into the engine (also what
# the aliases register as), and the voice cc-tts speaks in — a voice is
# REQUIRED on the wire, so this default is what the cockpit sends unless the
# operator picks another in Settings > Audio. Point cc-tts at another engine
# and set a voice IT knows. (CC_TTS_VOICE is in the app section above.)
#CC_SPEECH_TTS_MODEL=speaches-ai/Kokoro-82M-v1.0-ONNX
#CC_SPEECH_STT_MODEL=Systran/faster-whisper-small
# No public package index reachable? 1 makes the app phase install from
# requirements.lock instead of resolving, and turns the pypi/npm reachability
# probes into information rather than warnings. See deploy/AIRGAP.md.
CC_AIRGAP=0

# The sandbox IMAGE, per backend — these were literals in
# central_command/sandbox/runner.py until v2.43.0, which made a re-tagged or
# mirrored image an edit to app source. Defaults below are what the build
# scripts tag; change them only if you build or mirror the image yourself.
# Two refs for one image on purpose: the kubelet normalises a bare name to
# docker.io/library and cannot see a `localhost/` ref, while podman resolves
# `localhost/` out of local storage and would search registries for a bare name.
#CC_SANDBOX_IMAGE=localhost/cc-sandbox:1
#CC_SANDBOX_IMAGE_K8S=docker.io/library/cc-sandbox:1

# Tag of the locally-built graphiti image (build-graphiti-image.sh).
CC_GRAPHITI_TAG=1.0.2-anthropic

# ── Where every dependency comes from ──────────────────────────────────────
# `setup.sh fetch` (the phase right after preflight) acquires EVERYTHING the
# install needs — every third-party image (RESOLVED against the registry, see
# CC_IMG_* below), the three locally-built images, the Python resolution, the
# cockpit's npm tree — before anything is deployed, and STOPS (exit 3) on the
# first artifact it cannot get, naming the seam below that governs it. Fix the
# mirror and re-run — `deploy/discover.sh` maps what this network can reach and
# which mirrors to write here (it reads these same keys). Nothing falls back on
# its own; the choice is written here so a re-run and an update make the same one.
#
# Mirrors: blank = the public source. Only HOSTS are variables; a mirror that
# re-namespaces image PATHS needs the path edited in images.txt — that is
# deployment-time reality no variable can express.
#CC_REGISTRY_DOCKERIO=docker.io
#CC_REGISTRY_GHCR=ghcr.io
#CC_REGISTRY_MCR=mcr.microsoft.com
# Debian mirror for the two image builds that apt-get (graphiti, sandbox).
# Build containers do not see the host's apt config, so this goes in as a
# build-arg and REWRITES /etc/apt/sources.list.d/debian.sources from the
# base image's /etc/os-release (deb822; the slim images ship no
# sources.list at all). The main archive and the security archive are
# separate paths on every Debian mirror; leave the second blank to build
# without the security suite (fine for reproducible pins, not for CVE
# currency).
#CC_APT_MIRROR=https://MIRROR_HOST/debian
#CC_APT_SECURITY_MIRROR=https://MIRROR_HOST/debian-security
# PyPI simple index. Exported as BOTH PIP_INDEX_URL and UV_DEFAULT_INDEX —
# uv reads no PIP_* variable and no pip.conf (and UV_INDEX_URL is deprecated).
#CC_PYPI_INDEX_URL=https://MIRROR_HOST/pypi/simple
# Where uv downloads a CPython build when the host has no 3.12
# (UV_PYTHON_INSTALL_MIRROR; a file:// directory works).
#CC_PYTHON_MIRROR=https://MIRROR_HOST/python-build-standalone
# npm registry, for the cockpit's `npm ci` + build. Exported as
# NPM_CONFIG_REGISTRY. package-lock.json's resolved URLs point at
# registry.npmjs.org, which npm rewrites to this automatically (its
# `replace-registry-host` default).
#CC_NPM_REGISTRY=https://MIRROR_HOST/npm/
# Hugging Face hub, for the speech engine's model snapshots (HF_ENDPOINT
# inside the cc-speech pod; the pod does the pulling on its first boot). No
# mirror either? Pre-place the snapshots in the pod's volume — AIRGAP.md.
#CC_HF_ENDPOINT=https://MIRROR_HOST/huggingface
# Mandatory egress proxy for HOST-SIDE acquisition, fanned out to
# http(s)_proxy in both cases, with no_proxy pinned to loopback (every phase
# curls 127.0.0.1). podman forwards the proxy variables into builds on its
# own; credentials belong in ~/.netrc, never in this file. The CA that usually
# comes with an intercepting proxy is CC_CA_BUNDLE, in the app section above.
#CC_PROXY=http://proxy.corp.example:8080
# 1 = deploy/discover.sh passes --netrc on every probe, so credentials stay in
# ~/.netrc and never in this file.
#CC_NETRC=0
# 1 = TLS VERIFICATION OFF, everywhere this deployment can reach (v2.43.0,
# 2026-09-23 design record D4). There are exactly TWO trust knobs — this one and
# CC_CA_BUNDLE in the app section above — because per-tool knobs are what
# drifted. Prefer the CA: it keeps the supply chain verifiable. This one exists
# because a site may reasonably assume security through isolation instead, and
# then the honest thing is one documented switch rather than six undocumented
# ones. Every command that sees it prints ONE WARN line naming the consumers it
# drives; it is never a PASS and never silent.
#
# What each knob reaches (all names doc-verified 2026-09-23):
#   consumer        CA (CC_CA_BUNDLE)        insecure (CC_TLS_INSECURE=1)
#   curl (host)     CURL_CA_BUNDLE           `insecure` in <state>/curl/.curlrc
#                                             (CURL_HOME points there)
#   uv              SSL_CERT_FILE            UV_INSECURE_HOST=<index hosts>
#                   (UV_NATIVE_TLS is deprecated -> UV_SYSTEM_CERTS)
#   pip             PIP_CERT                 PIP_TRUSTED_HOST=<index hosts>
#   npm             NPM_CONFIG_CAFILE        NPM_CONFIG_STRICT_SSL=false
#   node            NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS      NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=0
#   git             GIT_SSL_CAINFO           GIT_SSL_NO_VERIFY=1
#   podman pulls    the machine's trust      `insecure = true` in the machine's
#                   store (./setup.sh          registries.conf drop-in, written
#                   machine writes it)        by ./setup.sh machine
#   podman build    --secret id=cc_ca,       --tls-verify=false
#                   installed into the         + CC_TLS_INSECURE=1 build-arg
#                   image's trust store
#   apt (in builds) the same build secret    Acquire::https::Verify-Peer "false"
#   LiteLLM         SSL_CERT_FILE            SSL_VERIFY=False
#   speech (HF)     REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE       NONE EXISTS — huggingface_hub has no
#                                             insecure switch. Use the CA, an HF
#                                             mirror (CC_HF_ENDPOINT), pre-placed
#                                             snapshots, or CC_ENABLE_SPEECH=0.
#CC_TLS_INSECURE=0
# A mirror deploy/discover.sh should probe for a resource that has no seam of
# its own above (KEY = the probe key upper-cased, '-'→'_': GITHUB, CRATES,
# MAVEN_CENTRAL…). Informational: it makes the report name your mirror.
#CC_DISCO_MIRROR_GITHUB=https://MIRROR_HOST/github

# ── Image refs: RESOLVER-OWNED, do not hand-edit ────────────────────────────
# `resolve-images.sh` (run by `setup.sh fetch`) writes one CC_IMG_<NAME> line
# per third-party image and compose.yaml reads them. Each name comes from the
# image path — CC_IMG_POSTGRES, CC_IMG_NEO4J, CC_IMG_BERRIAI_LITELLM_DATABASE.
#
# What it resolves against is images.txt, which carries THREE tiers per image:
#   constraint   the version SERIES this release supports (16, 5.26, 7,
#                main-stable); a substitute must also keep the locked tag's
#                flavour (7-alpine admits 7.4-alpine, never 7.4-alpine3.22)
#   locked tag   the tag this release was tested against
#   locked digest what that tag must hash to — or `-` when the tag is ROLLING
#                (a series or a channel: 16, 7-alpine, main-stable), which
#                moves on every upstream rebuild
# Locked tag present  -> used, and its digest is verified (a mismatch FAILS:
#                        the tag has drifted or been poisoned). A rolling tag
#                        has no digest to verify; the tag is the pin.
# Locked tag absent   -> the newest tag satisfying the constraint is
#                        substituted, with a WARN naming it. Untested but
#                        supported: a green `./setup.sh verify` is what makes
#                        it so — capability is proven by probes, not versions.
# Nothing satisfies   -> FAIL naming the constraint and what the mirror has.
# The result is recorded in $CC_STATE_DIR/installed.manifest (ref, tag, digest,
# locked|substituted, when) — the provenance, and the rollback record.
#
# AN OPERATOR PIN WINS (v2.43.0, 2026-09-23 design record D2). Set a
# CC_IMG_<NAME> here yourself and the resolver treats it as authoritative: it
# VERIFIES that exact ref exists on its registry (a manifest HEAD by tag, or by
# digest for a `...@sha256:...` ref — parsed from YOUR ref, so a mirror that
# re-namespaces the PATH works), prints `WARN <name>: operator pin honoured`,
# records it in <state>/installed.manifest as `pinned`, and never rewrites it.
# A pin the registry does not have is a FAIL naming this key — a pin is checked,
# not trusted. Unset the key to go back to resolving against images.txt.
# This is THE seam for a path-renaming mirror, and for "use this tag, I checked".
# How the resolver tells your pin from its own last write: the manifest records
# what it wrote, so a value here that differs from that record is yours.
#
# The three locally BUILT images resolve their base through the same keys, so a
# base substitution or a renamed base path needs no Dockerfile edit:
#   CC_IMG_ZEPAI_KNOWLEDGE_GRAPH_MCP  graphiti's base
#   CC_IMG_PYTHON                     the sandbox's base
#   CC_IMG_PLAYWRIGHT_PYTHON          the crawler's base

# ── Generated by make-secrets.sh if left blank — see the header ─────────────
# Not CC_-prefixed: each is the name the CONTAINER reads.
LITELLM_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# OPTIONAL, never generated — LiteLLM admin-UI login. Unset means the UI logs
# in as username `admin` with CC_LLM_PROXY_ADMIN_KEY as the password.
#UI_USERNAME=
#UI_PASSWORD=

# Credentials Graphiti uses to reach LiteLLM. On a real install these should
# be LiteLLM VIRTUAL keys, each scoped to one model group, so a leak in one
# cannot spend through another. make-secrets.sh falls back to the master key
# when they are blank, which works but is coarser.
GRAPHITI_LLM_API_KEY=
EMBEDDER_API_KEY=
RERANKER_API_KEY=

# ── Only used when the n8n profile is played (CC_ENABLE_N8N=1) ──────────────
N8N_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
N8N_DB_USER=n8n
N8N_DB_PASSWORD=
N8N_DB_NAME=n8n
# ===== end deployment section ================================================
